WEBVTT

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.350
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.310
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.570
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.950
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:17.200
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.710
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.510
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.430
Yakitate!! Japan

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:46.070
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:53.900
Under the night sky, we, who are whistling...

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:01.660
without words, are just tracing constellations with our fingers.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:09.130
The dreams of the cold-sensitive... Your cold hands...

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:18.510
The warming magic is to believe in one path.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:27.270
The single star found on the other side of the comet cloud...

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.530
whether it be a shining or a faint star...

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.490
is the light of only you.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.270
On the other side of the cloud in your heart,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:43.080
is a sign post that remains unseen.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:50.800
Now, opening these hands, what will you believe in today?

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.010
Pantasia’s rival bakery, St. Pierre.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.590
Matsushiro, who accepted the challenge of the
main store manager, Mokoyama Tsuyoshi,

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.060
entered a bet involving Kazuma in a new
bread showdown to be shown on TV.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:07.470
That was...

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.810
an attempt to rescue Pantasia’s southern
Tokyo store from its dwindling sales.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.030
A major battle of great risk.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.860
By the way, what is the price of that bread?

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.320
Price?

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.780
I haven’t really thought about it.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.490
In that case, please give it some thought.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.910
The general manager of the main store,
Meister Kirisaki, who had suddenly appeared...

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.220
also had confidence in Kazuma’s
new bread creation. However...

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.050
New
Bread Showdown
St. Pierre
vs
Pantasia

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.260
They say that two giants cannot stand together.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.550
Here, in this place, I shall bring Pantasia to its end.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:46.520
H-Hello.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.440
No. It may not work.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.100
Azuma’s bread...

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.940
<i>Japan</i> #57, has...

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.700
a fatal flaw.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.710
Hattori Surprise!!

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.880
Transformation With the Secret Sauce!

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.450
It has begun! TV-Grand Prix Special.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.300
St. Pierre vs Pantasia,
new bread showdown!

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.060
Pantasia

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.780
From a great bakery chain,
St. Pierre’s main Tokyo store...

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.570
The manager, “God of sweetened buns...”

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:35.870
Mokoyama Tsuyoshi!

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.830
On the opposing side, representing
Pantasia’s southern Tokyo store...

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.880
Azuma Kazuma!

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.590
The new bread creations of the two have
already been brought on stage.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.430
Win, win, Manager!

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.090
Fight! Yeah!
Main Tokyo Store Manager
Mokoyama Tsuyoshi
St. Pierre

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.640
Oh no, you need not worry.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.150
Just watch, Ken-chan.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:11.230
I will completely defeat
your apprentice boy.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.040
Do your best, cutie!

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.160
Will he be all right?

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:17.370
Hattori Yukio
Akagawa Kyouko
Minokami Kouji

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:21.410
Now then, let us introduce the judges
who will be trialing the new breads.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.370
The head of the Hattori College of Nutrition,

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.420
The head of the Hattori College of Nutrition,
Hattori Yukio-sensei.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.000
That’s me.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.090
President of the Bread Industry Newspaper,
Akagawa Kyouko-san.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:34.470
And the producer of this show,
Minokami Kouji.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.470
I wait in anticipation.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.930
Today, we will be displaying the scores after
the judges have each had a sample.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.180
With a maximum of 10 points from each judge,
victory will be decided by the total points.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.270
Now, what you’ve all been waiting for...

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.760
Let’s start by announcing St. Pierre’s new bread.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:57.520
Yes! Have a look at this!

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.690
It appears to be a round bread.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.160
The bread is being brought to the judges.
Akagawa Kyouko
Hattori Yukio
Minokami Kouji

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.210
This is...

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.910
Note: “Kouign-amann” is a form of pastry from the Brittany region of France, made with a very large proportion of butter.
At a glance, it seems to be a regular kouign-amann.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.670
Kouign-amann.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.800
It’s French for “butter cake.”

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.930
The bread dough contains a lot of sugar and butter.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.560
It’s a traditional sweet from the Brittany region.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:26.900
However, these days it’s
not so uncommon...

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.450
Can he really call this a new creation?

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.470
Now then, please start the tasting.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.730
Sweet!

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.530
This sweet, but also salty taste...

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.370
Are the ingredients salt-free butter,
rock salt, and sugar?

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.200
This is truly rich, with plenty of depth.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.670
The outside is surprisingly hard,
but it has been caramelized.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:53.240
Nevertheless...

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.920
Normally it would have a filling of “niringo,”

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.300
but this kouign-amann is different.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.340
Definitely, the moment I put it in my
mouth it seemed too sweet, but...

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.680
That’s right.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.770
The more I chew it, the more it
has a soft, smooth sensation.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.760
It transforms into a natural sweetness.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.190
This... this sensation is...

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:15.100
That’s because there’s a secret inside!

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:16.390
“Secret,” he says?

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.100
A secret?

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.460
This taste...

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:21.110
This filling...

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:24.980
Pudding!

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:25.860
It’s the taste of pudding!

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.560
That is correct!

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.620
However, the custard pudding inside contains no sugar!

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.480
Oh, I see.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.190
If both the outside and inside
were sweet,

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.450
it would be too sweet, and ruin the individual tastes.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.200
But with a pudding that isn’t sweet,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.080
the moment it mixes in my mouth
with the overly sweet dough,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:48.130
it transforms into a faint, but
pleasantly creamy sweetness.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:49.750
This is wonderful.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:51.590
It’s certainly a new kind of sweetness.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.880
This is a new bread worthy of St. Pierre’s pride...

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.640
Kouign-pudding-amann!

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:58.840
Kouign...

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.350
pudding...

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.100
amann!

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:02.510
Minokami Kouji

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.360
This is delicious. 10 points.
Minokami Kouji

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.320
Minokami Kouji

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:05.810
Akagawa Kyouko

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:06.560
Same for me.
Akagawa Kyouko

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:06.570
Très bien!
Same for me.
Akagawa Kyouko

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:07.820
Hattori Yukio
Très bien!

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.400
10 points, 10 points, 10 points...
30 points!

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.660
Amazing, it’s a perfect score
straight away!

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:15.950
P-Perfect score?!

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:18.540
Ohh... my southern Tokyo store...

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.460
Boy, I got a perfect score.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:26.380
Looks like my win has already been decided,
even before you announce your <i>Japan</i>.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:28.380
Really?

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.680
Of course it has, stupid boy!

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:32.930
Oh well.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.390
Hurry up and announce your <i>Japan</i>, get humiliated,

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.890
and go home!

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.020
P-Perfect score...

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:43.150
If Azuma’s <i>Japan</i> #57 has a fatal flaw,

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.360
like the manager said...

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.150
we’re already finished.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:54.620
Now, moving on to announce Pantasia’s new bread.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:00.710
Oh?

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:04.510
The surface is the same, but there are different shapes.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.210
Ah, it is now being brought to the judges.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:12.090
Hmm... the shapes and sizes are different.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.980
I wonder if the inside is the same?

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.020
At a glance, they appear to be fried.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:20.970
If you eat it, you’ll understand.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.070
Tasting...

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:36.660
This is...?

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.500
Note: “Hinoki” is Japanese cypress wood, and is used to build stages for classical theater.
Why are we standing on a hinoki stage?

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:01.980
Delicious.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:06.150
Delicious, this taste is...

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:08.230
Kabuki...

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:08.940
age...

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.570
It is...

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:20.080
Kabukiage? The rice cracker?

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.540
It goes with bread?

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.120
I-I wonder.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:25.960
Tasty. It works.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.750
The inside is plain bread, but it has such depth.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.750
And it really goes with the sweet sake
and soy sauce-flavored coating.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:35.630
What? It seems like a success.

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:37.090
What on earth is that bread?

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.550
You... Azuma-kun, from Pantasia.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:41.350
This bread is...?

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.350
Kabukiage bread, <i>Japan</i> #57!

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:44.770
Hattori Yukio
Akagawa Kyouko
Minokami Kouji

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:45.680
<i>J-Japan</i>?

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:46.940
I haven’t heard of if.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:48.140
Yeah.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:52.940
I took our store’s leftover bread,
put it in a special sauce, and fried it.

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:03.000
L-Leftover?

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:05.250
Even though it’s this soft?

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.250
Our leftover bread lasts for days,

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:11.580
and only the surface that’s in contact
with the air becomes hard.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.210
It’s perfect for frying the surface to a crisp.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.130
The idea aside,

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:20.590
I never would have thought this
to be Pantasia’s leftover bread.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.430
I feel as though I have witnessed
Pantasia’s hidden power.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:24.760
Let me try it!

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:25.850
Give me a bite!

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:30.140
Following St. Pierre, the judges
are astounded once again.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.560
We can expect high scores.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.900
I see.

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.400
The reason Azuma was collecting the leftover bread,

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:36.940
was for this.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.490
We can do it.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:39.990
I think we can do it.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.160
There’s no flaw.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.290
Manager, don’t surprise us like that.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:46.200
Manager?

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.210
Wait just a minute!

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:55.260
Everybody, please think about this more rationally.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.510
No matter how good it tastes...

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.720
it’s leftover bread!

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.430
Leftover?

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:03.720
Oh yeah, he did say that.

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:06.440
Is it really okay
to use something like that?

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.480
Leftovers that should normally be thrown away...

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.730
To put those back in the store
as a product...

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:18.370
I can only think that Pantasia is treating
its customers with disrespect!

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.080
So he also noticed... that Mokoyama.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:29.660
Yes, this lousy image is <i>Japan</i> #57’s flaw.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.800
I can certainly understand
St. Pierre’s view.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.590
Yeah, it doesn’t matter how tasty it is.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.420
They’re leftovers, after all.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:38.180
It’s strange, don’t you think?

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.100
Yeah.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:40.940
But...

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:46.490
But, the southern Tokyo store’s bread is delicious, no matter how many days pass!

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:51.070
Yes, our bread tastes good, no matter
how many days may pass.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:53.400
I do understand that.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:54.610
I’m confident in it.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:56.200
But you know, Azuma...

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.710
Customers don’t know any of that.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.660
What you are trying to do...

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.910
is a taboo of the worst kind, for a bakery.

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:09.550
Do you still intend to use
that leftover bread?

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:11.910
I will.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:14.130
Bread this tasty...

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:16.760
is being thrown away, without ever being eaten.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.770
It’s so wasteful, I can’t stand it.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:24.340
All this great bread... that everyone
put their effort into making...

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:29.070
Back when I had just joined the Pantasia main store...

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.570
throwing out the leftovers each day...

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:34.740
was the job I hated most.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.990
To sell everything was impossible.

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.740
I knew that, but...

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.490
I always thought about it.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:49.010
Was there no way for us to avoid throwing
out the bread we put our hearts into?

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:52.340
I thought something like that was just a dream.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:54.080
Manager.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:55.470
Manager.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:13:00.220
You knew, didn’t you?
Why didn’t you stop him?

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:03.220
Why didn’t you get Azuma to make
a new bread of the usual kind?

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.340
The one you ate was the manager’s flavor.

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:07.200
Yours was Tsukino’s flavor.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:08.350
Yours was Kawachi’s flavor.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:10.440
M-My flavor?

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:14.410
Azuma didn’t want to waste your bread,
or my bread, or Tsukino’s bread...

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:16.820
because they all taste so good.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.480
That’s what he was thinking.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.860
Our bread...

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:23.920
Once I knew Azuma felt that way...

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.370
I couldn’t say no to him.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.300
I’m sorry, guys.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:34.340
They were delicious, right?
You said they were delicious, didn’t you?

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.460
I understand how you feel,

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:38.920
but Mokoyama-san’s point is valid.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.300
I have to take it into consideration for your score.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:43.090
No way...

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:44.390
Azuma...

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:45.600
Azuma-san...

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:51.140
It was a double-edged sword,
but thinking that it might revive the store,

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.150
I bet everything on Azuma...

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.240
Perhaps it was reckless, after all.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:13:58.990
Please hold on a moment.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:04.860
You have not asked an important question yet.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.590
Meister Kirisaki.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:21.750
Please hold on a moment.

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.340
You have not asked an important question yet.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.470
Before announcing the score, I would like
you to ask the price of both creations.

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:31.640
W...

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:32.270
Wh...

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:32.930
Who?

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:34.930
It’s the main store’s...!

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.190
H-He is...

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.110
Pantasia’s main store general manager...

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:41.990
Meister Kirisaki.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:46.110
Having turned down so many
invitations from the media,

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:50.250
the man who is said to never reveal himself in public...

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.320
Why is he here?

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:55.580
Oh! It’s the masked man from yesterday!

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:02.960
It’s an honor to be in the presence
of Pantasia’s supreme artisan.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.550
But even if you have no way of winning,

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:07.170
I will not accept you interfering with this competition!

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:10.100
I have no intention of interfering with the competition.

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:13.980
Only... Aside from being placed
in store as a new product,

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:18.320
I believe the price should be taken into consideration for scoring, in this case...

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:23.190
Being a man who loves bread!

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:28.370
Yes, he certainly has a point.

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:31.660
Well... in that case,
starting with St. Pierre’s new bread.

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.490
Unless there’s a problem,
may we ask what the price will be?

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.250
No problem.

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:42.460
I don’t know what he’s plotting, but there’s no chance.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.920
As a matter of fact, this new bread,
kouign-pudding-amann...

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.420
is loaded with extraordinary ingredients.

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:52.590
For the custard pudding I used an extravagant amount of gifu-jidori eggs...

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:56.640
For the sweetness of the dough I used
high-grade granulated sugar,

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:58.740
which brings out the greatest amount of natural taste

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.770
through its large grains and perfect sweetness.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:07.520
It is a kouign-amann designed with the clarity and refinement of sweetness in mind!

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.450
I was particular about the ingredients for the pudding.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.610
There’s the flour,
and the finest butter, so...

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:16.370
If a standard kouign-amann would be 160 yen...

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:20.290
Including the cost of the pudding,
I would ask for 260 yen.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:22.630
But wait a minute.

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:26.790
If he’s asking about the price, it means
he’s confident that he can go cheaper.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:33.010
I’ll compete for the lowest possible price, too.

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:34.430
It’s decided!

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:36.720
I will make it 190 yen!

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:41.020
Cheap! It’s too cheap!

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:44.730
Even a regular kouign-amann costs 150 or 160 yen.

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:48.060
Oh? That is surprisingly cheap.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.320
I will also have one, at some time.

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:53.360
Isn’t it, isn’t it?

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:55.030
Now, Pantasia is next.

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.170
Your price, how much?

00:16:58.330 --> 00:16:59.580
Azuma-kun.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:06.260
This kabukiage bread, <i>Japan</i> #57’s price...

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:10.050
is 0 yen.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.530
0 yen? 0 yen?! Nothing?!

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:15.050
Free, he says?

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:16.050
What’s that supposed to mean?

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.920
That’s cheating, cheating!

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:21.730
Even if you don’t want to lose,

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:24.860
charging 0 yen out of desperation is just dirty!

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:27.610
That’s not true.

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:29.900
They were leftovers to begin with,

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:34.070
and I just wanted everyone to know the
taste of the southern Tokyo store.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:35.030
That’s all.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.160
But despite that, how can you charge 0?

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:41.460
But, that’s...

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:46.040
Like I said...

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:50.920
Matsushiro-san.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:55.350
The rest is up to you.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:58.690
I see.

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:00.060
Manager?

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.310
This may be sudden,
but please excuse me!

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:05.690
I’m Matsushiro, the manager of Pantasia’s
southern Tokyo store,

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:09.270
which is located diagonally opposite
from St. Pierre’s main store.

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:11.150
M-Manager?

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:14.150
We have always been

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:18.030
producing high-quality bread that
does not lose to the main store,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:21.580
but we’re a small-scale branch
with a mere 5 employees...

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:25.580
and in reality, we just don’t seem
to get noticed by the public.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.170
And so...!

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:29.380
The 0 yen bread that Azuma here just proposed

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:33.630
is to show everyone the taste
of the southern Tokyo store...

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.300
<i>Japan</i> #57 has been announced.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.100
This is certainly not because we thought we would lose,

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:42.060
or that we just thought of it and
decided to make it 0 yen,

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.680
or any such case.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.930
I only thought up the price just now.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:48.940
W-What?

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:50.440
At any rate,

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:54.110
that <i>Japan</i> #57 will be in the store starting tomorrow.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:55.530
Thank you very much.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:00.030
Hey. You guys stand and bow, too.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:02.530
U-Um...

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:06.040
Please visit Pantasia’s
southern Tokyo store.

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:15.340
So perhaps the manager’s flavor that boy mentioned...

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:18.090
He must have used that masculine man’s bread.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.800
I kind of have a thing for
rough-looking men.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:21.930
Maybe I’ll try some.

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:23.800
There was also a Tsukino-flavor, wasn’t there?

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.100
That would, um, be me.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:28.810
Please stop by sometime.

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.310
C-Cute! I want some, too!

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:32.810
Me, too!

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:34.730
Show some more appeal.

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:37.690
Give them a pose, and make it sexy, sexy!

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:39.240
It’s for the store, so do it.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:40.570
Sex appeal...

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:44.000
Tsu... Tsukino-flavor... come and try some, okay?

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.170
Hey, wasn’t there another one?

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:53.790
That would be my flavor.

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:55.790
It’s Kawachi-flavor, Kawachi-flavor.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:57.800
Note: “Kakashi” means a figurehead or dummy. Alternatively, a scarecrow.
Was it kakashi-flavor?

00:19:57.800 --> 00:19:59.140
Wha–?

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:01.300
Fine, I have nothing to say!

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:06.300
In that case, after considering the price,
we will judge Pantasia’s bread.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:15.770
10 points...

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:17.020
10 points...

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:18.860
10 points!

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:21.280
A perfect score!

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:24.360
This is mortifying!

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:24.370
St. Pierre
Mokoyama Tsuyoshi
Main Tokyo Store Manager
This is mortifying!

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:25.870
No way!
Main Tokyo Store Manager
Mokoyama Tsuyoshi
St. Pierre

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.120
W-We’re saved.

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:31.870
Yes. Now it seems that my southern
Tokyo store won’t be ruined.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:36.880
It’s as if he knew this would happen
all along, it’s irritating.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:38.470
However, just this time...

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:53.690
It has been decided, this new bread
showdown... ends in a draw!

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:54.730
Southern Tokyo Store
Main Store
St. Pierre
Pantasia
TV Grand Prix Special

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:13.830
Wow, I was really worried at one point.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.000
Now we won’t lose our jobs.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:20.170
Well, since you drew with the enemy
manager, we’ve kept our reputation.

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:21.720
Right, Azuma?

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.010
I thought this would happen from the beginning.

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:27.010
Or so you say.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:29.890
Right! Let’s celebrate tonight!

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.310
Manager, it’s still business hours.

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.680
Don’t worry about small details,
we’re going.

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:35.310
Okay.

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:35.940
We’re taking a break.

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:36.560
Okay!
We’re taking a break.

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:43.360
Got it, people?

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:45.070
The owner is in America right now.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.740
This incident must be kept secret.

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:51.580
If you expose this, I will make you all my lovers!

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:55.790
This bread looks delicious.

00:21:55.790 --> 00:21:57.000
Isn’t there any Manager’s flavor?

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.790
Hey, why isn’t there any Tsukino-flavor?

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:00.380
a
W-What is this?

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:02.000
Hey, I want Tsukino-flavor, Tsukino-flavor...
a
W-What is this?

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.590
Why do I always have to look after the store alone?
Hey, I want Tsukino-flavor, Tsukino-flavor...

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:06.930
Come to think of it, didn’t they
say that it starts tomorrow?

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:10.050
Okay, moving on!

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:11.260
Sheesh...

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:13.220
Manager, I’m suffering.

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:15.270
This is so messed up.

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:16.890
Hey, Kawachi.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:19.020
Haven’t we forgotten something?

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:20.150
Nope, not at all.

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:23.990
I feel like we’ve forgotten something.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:26.060
Oh well.

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:27.900
Everyone...

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:34.040
come back soon!

00:22:38.570 --> 00:22:46.710
If I think of you, and even utter the clichéd phrase “I love you,”

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:55.720
it makes such a nice sound, and you continue talking.

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:06.730
The hopeless tears your bold self sometimes shows me form flag of truce.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:11.900
If nothing is said, then all the more,

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:18.040
I just can’t look away. Things like sadness and pain...

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:20.610
Well, that happens a lot in life, after all.

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:24.750
When you casually say, “Do your best,” it gives me strength.

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:29.520
Whenever your small back trembles, I will embrace you right away.

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:33.360
Even if the scenery outside my window changes...

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:38.060
Stay with me as you are, bad sleeping habits and all.

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:43.230
If you sulk, and utter

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:52.040
Even the clichéd “I hate you,” it comes across as the opposite.

00:23:52.440 --> 00:24:01.400
I will love you, no matter what. It’s just another Sunday to me.

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:09.750
New recruit of Pantasia or not, as
I am now, I cannot beat Azuma.

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:12.380
Kawachi-san, do you want Solar Hands?

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:15.090
I want them! But I don’t like this special training!

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:16.640
Next episode, Yakitate!! Japan.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:19.060
Kawachi Kai!!
The Solar Gauntlet!

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:20.050
Be sure to see it!

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.810
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.690
“Purin” isn’t called “purin” because it
goes “purin purin” when it shakes.

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:28.980
The English word “pudding” just sounded
like “purin” to the Japanese.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:31.820
As with “waishatsu” and others, it’s Japlish!

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:33.950
I see!
